# ulmo 0.11.0

This release builds on 0.10.0's database-free compiler with native AMD64
support, reproducible self-hosting on both targets, a smaller compulsory
runtime, and a conventional Linux installation layout.

## Native AMD64 and i386

- Added the AMD64 backend and ELF64 output. The compiler and its libraries
  now build and run natively on either architecture.
- Included separate static bootstrap compilers for AMD64 and i386. An
  AMD64 build does not require kernel support for 32-bit executables.
- Added three-stage builds: `make check` verifies that the stage-2 and
  stage-3 compiler and object-generator binaries are identical.
- Added `HUGEINT`, a 64-bit integer type on both targets. It uses register
  pairs on i386 and native 64-bit registers on AMD64. Constant sums,
  differences and comparisons are folded by the compiler.
- Fixed AMD64 instruction encoding, including REX prefixes, immediate
  widths and the 16-bit operations used for floating-point rounding.

## Smaller statically linked programs

The compiler continues to link and initialize whole modules. The size
reduction comes from removing compulsory dependencies, rather than
procedure-level smart linking or a shared runtime library.

The following sizes are in bytes, after stripping:

| empty program | AMD64 | i386 |
|---|---:|---:|
| before dependency reductions | 741,272 | 545,408 |
| 0.11.0 | 576,504 | 425,916 |
| reduction | 164,768 (22.2%) | 119,492 (21.9%) |

The earlier figures are the development baseline before the dependency
reductions, not an AMD64 build of 0.10.0. The minimal dependency closure
has fallen from 81 runtime modules to 69.

### Choosing Out or Write

`Out` is a new, lightweight module for buffered strings, characters,
integers and line breaks. Its interface is `Char`, `String`,
`Int(value, width)`, `Line` and `Ln`.

| program | AMD64 | i386 | added over empty, AMD64 / i386 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| empty | 576,504 | 425,916 | 0 / 0 |
| hello world using `Out` | 578,064 | 426,932 | 1,560 / 1,016 |
| hello world using `Write` | 640,080 | 471,888 | 63,576 / 45,972 |

Importing `Write` increases the resulting executable size because its
floating-point output brings in the general formatter. Importing `Out`
adds only about 1-1.6 KB to the minimal program. The existing `Write`
interface remains available; use `Write` or `Print` when richer output is
needed.

```oberon
MODULE Hello;
   IMPORT Out;
BEGIN
   Out.Line("Hello, world!");
END Hello.
```

Core diagnostics now share `BasicWrite`; process naming no longer imports
the argument parser; unused socket and timeout imports have been removed.
The native timezone provider is selected automatically when `Timezones`
appears in the linked dependency closure.

## Runtime and library fixes

- Added native AMD64 socket calls and correct memory-mapping byte offsets.
- Converted AMD64 kernel resource-limit and C structure layouts without
  overwriting caller buffers or truncating pointer fields.
- Fixed basic I/O syscall numbers, parent-PID lookup, descriptor
  duplication and fixed-width IPv6 address fields.
- Fixed timezone-file byte order, zoneinfo paths, long `TZ` names and
  unsigned transition/abbreviation indexes.
- Hardened coroutine stack growth, signal delivery and garbage collection,
  including allocation during critical regions and stack overflow while
  objects are being copied.
- Corrected runtime-error source locations and messages, including index
  bounds and NIL dereferences.
- Added 13 runtime regression programs, run with `make check-runtime`.

## Build, installation and compiled-file reuse

```sh
make ARCH=amd64
make ARCH=amd64 check check-runtime
make install PREFIX=/usr LIBDIR=/usr/lib64 DESTDIR="$pkgdir"
```

The public command is installed in `/usr/bin/ulmo`; private compilers,
tools and libraries live under `/usr/lib64/ulmo/amd64` (or the configured
library directory); source interfaces and implementations live under
`/usr/share/ulmo/src`. Use `ARCH=i386` for the 32-bit target.

Compiled library interfaces are installed alongside the libraries. The
compiler checks source and import fingerprints before reusing cached
objects, and compiles the implementations of imported user modules when
linking. Library modules supplied explicitly as source take precedence
over installed copies.

The sources are organized as `src/rtl`, `src/lib` and `src/compiler`.
The old database-based tools remain optional through `make cdb-tools`.

## Current limits

- Linux on AMD64 or 32-bit x86. Normal i386 programs require a
  Pentium/i586-class CPU because allocation uses `CMPXCHG8B`; floating
  point uses x87, with no MMX or SSE requirement.
- AMD64 addresses are 64-bit, but `INTEGER`, `LONGINT` and sizes remain
  32-bit. Code/static data use the small code model, and the managed
  allocator remains restricted to low addresses.
- The AMD64 System V IPC interface and full-width syscall results still
  need further porting. Supporting more than 2 GB of managed address
  space and a future 386+387 target are recorded in `WANTED`.
- Programs are static executables with a writable/executable segment;
  source-level DWARF debugging is not available.

## Verification

`make check check-runtime` passed on AMD64 and i386, including identical
stage-2/stage-3 binaries. Staged Linux installation and compilation of an
installed `Out` program were also verified.

Full changes: https://github.com/norayr/ulmo/compare/v0.10.0...v0.11.0
